---

<LegalNoticeLayout
  title="Cookie notice"
  description="Information about cookies and similar technologies used on rjmlaird.co.uk."
  eyebrow="Legal"
  lead="This notice explains how cookies and similar technologies may be used on this website, what they do and how you can manage your choices."
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  sections={s.list}
  related={["privacy", "terms", "accessibility", "booking"]}
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    <p>
      Essential technologies may be used to keep the site secure and working.
      Optional analytics, preferences, embedded content and marketing
      technologies should be used only in line with the choices available to
      you.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.overview}>

    <p>
      This cookie notice explains how <a href={siteLegal.siteUrl}>{siteLegal.siteUrl}</a> uses cookies, local
      storage, pixels, scripts, device identifiers and related technologies.
      For simplicity, this notice refers to all of these as “cookies” unless a
      distinction matters.
    </p>

    <p>
      Cookies can be set directly by this website, known as first-party
      cookies, or by another organisation whose service is used on the site,
      known as third-party cookies.
    </p>

    <p>
      This notice should be read alongside the <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>, which explains how personal
      information is handled more generally.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by["what-are-cookies"]}>

    <p>
      Cookies are small text files that a website can store on your device, or
      read from it, when you visit. They can help a website remember information
      about your visit, such as a preference you have selected, whether you have
      seen a notice or how a feature should work.
    </p>

    <p>
      Similar technologies can include local storage, session storage, pixels,
      tags, scripts, software-development kits, browser identifiers and
      device-level information. They can be used for similar purposes, including
      security, preferences, analytics, embedded content and advertising.
    </p>

    <p>
      Cookies can be session cookies, which normally expire when you close your
      browser, or persistent cookies, which remain for a specified period or
      until you delete them.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by["how-we-use-them"]}>

    <p>
      Depending on the features enabled on the site, cookies may be used for the
      following purposes:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Essential operation:</strong> maintaining security, preventing
        abuse, remembering a cookie choice and delivering core website
        functionality.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Preferences:</strong> remembering choices such as appearance,
        language, accessibility or display preferences where those features are
        available.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Analytics:</strong> understanding how visitors find and use the
        site, identifying technical problems and improving content, navigation
        and performance.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Embedded content:</strong> loading third-party features such as
        video, maps, social-media posts, forms, calendars or booking tools.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Marketing:</strong> measuring advertising, building audiences,
        limiting repeated adverts or supporting remarketing, only if such tools
        are used and you make the relevant choice.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The actual technologies used are listed in the <a href={s.by["cookie-inventory"].href}>cookie inventory</a>. This list must be kept
      up to date whenever a tool, embed, plugin, tag or service is added,
      removed or reconfigured.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.consent}>

    <p>
      Some cookies are strictly necessary for the website, its security or a
      service you have specifically requested. These may operate without asking
      for consent because the site cannot provide the relevant function without
      them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Other cookies may require your consent before they are set or read. Where
      consent is required, optional technologies should not load until you have
      made a clear positive choice to allow them.
    </p>

    <p>
      You should be able to accept, reject or manage optional cookie categories
      with equal ease. Rejecting optional cookies should not prevent you from
      accessing the ordinary public content of this website.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can change or withdraw a previous optional-cookie choice at any time
      using the control below.
    </p>

    <CookiePreferences>
        <p>
        TODO: Replace this placeholder with your real consent-management control.
        For example, connect this button to your consent platform’s “open
        preferences” function.
      </p>

        <Fragment slot="after">
          <p class="small-print">
        If your site does not use optional cookies, remove this control and
        update this notice to state that only essential technologies are used.
      </p>
        </Fragment>
      </CookiePreferences>

    <p>
      Where an analytics or preference technology can lawfully operate using an
      exemption rather than consent, you should still provide clear information
      and a simple, free way to object where the exemption requires it.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.essential}>

    <p>
      Essential technologies support functions that are necessary for the
      website to operate, remain secure or deliver a service you specifically
      request. They may include technologies used for:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Remembering your cookie choices</li>
      <li>Security, fraud prevention and rate limiting</li>
      <li>Load balancing, network management and website delivery</li>
      <li>Preventing malicious or automated form submissions</li>
      <li>Maintaining a requested session or interaction</li>
      <li>Basic accessibility or display settings necessary to provide the selected service</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Essential does not simply mean useful to the website operator. A
      technology should be treated as essential only where it is genuinely
      necessary for the relevant service or security function. 
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.analytics}>

    <p>
      Analytics can help show how visitors reach the site, which pages are
      useful, how content performs and whether technical issues affect use. For
      example, analytics may measure page views, referral source, device or
      browser type, broad location, navigation paths, load times and
      interaction with site content.
    </p>

    <p>
      Analytics should be configured to minimise data collection, avoid
      unnecessary identification, avoid advertising and cross-site tracking,
      restrict access and apply suitable retention settings.
    </p>

    <p>
      TODO: State the analytics provider, whether analytics runs only after
      consent or under a permitted statistical-purpose exemption, the data
      collected, how it is minimised, whether IP addresses are truncated or
      transformed, whether data is shared, and how visitors can opt out.
    </p>

    <p>
      If analytics is used solely for aggregate service improvement and meets
      all relevant exemption conditions, clear information and an easy opt-out
      may still be required. If it is also used for advertising, profiling,
      cross-site tracking or other non-exempt purposes, consent is required
      before it operates.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.preferences}>

    <p>
      Preference technologies may remember choices you make, such as a display
      theme, contrast setting, text preference, language, layout or other
      appearance and functionality options.
    </p>

    <p>
      TODO: Describe the actual preferences offered by the site, the technology
      used and whether it operates only after consent or under a lawful
      appearance/functionality exemption with a simple opt-out.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can also use browser and operating-system settings to control many
      appearance and accessibility preferences independently of this website.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by["embedded-content"]}>

    <p>
      Some pages may include content or functionality supplied by third parties,
      such as video players, maps, social-media posts, calendar tools, forms,
      newsletters, audio players or booking systems.
    </p>

    <p>
      Third-party embeds can set their own cookies or access information on your
      device. Where this is not essential, the embed should not load until you
      choose to allow the relevant category, or you choose to load that specific
      embed.
    </p>

    <p>
      A privacy-friendly alternative may be used where practical. For example, a
      page can show a preview and a “Load content” button rather than loading an
      external video or social-media embed automatically.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can usually access the underlying third-party service directly by
      following a normal link instead of loading an embedded version.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.marketing}>

    <p>
      Marketing technologies may be used to measure advertising, build
      audiences, track conversions, limit repeated advertising or show more
      relevant advertising on other platforms.
    </p>

    <p>
      TODO: If you do not use advertising, remarketing, social-media pixels,
      conversion tracking or audience-building tools, replace this section with:
      “This website does not currently use marketing or advertising cookies.”
    </p>

    <p>
      If these technologies are used, they must not be set or read until you
      have given the required consent. Declining marketing cookies should not
      affect your ability to access ordinary public website content.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by["third-parties"]}>

    <p>
      This website may link to or integrate with independent services. Depending
      on what is active, these may include:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Website hosting, content-delivery and security providers</li>
      <li>Analytics and consent-management providers</li>
      <li>Newsletter, email or form providers</li>
      <li>Booking, calendar and payment providers</li>
      <li>Video, audio, mapping or document-hosting providers</li>
      <li>Social-media platforms and external publications</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Independent providers are responsible for their own cookies, privacy
      information, security arrangements and terms. Before using an external
      service or supplying it with personal information, review its own notices.
    </p>

    <p>
      TODO: Add named providers and links to their privacy or cookie notices
      where they set or read technologies through this website.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by["cookie-inventory"]}>

    <p>
      The table below should list every cookie and similar technology used by
      this website, including first-party and third-party tools. It should be
      updated after every significant website, tag-manager, embed, analytics,
      hosting or consent-platform change.
    </p>

    <DataTable caption="Cookie inventory" columns={cookieColumns} rows={cookieInventory} minWidth="78rem" />

    <p>
      TODO: Audit the live site in a clean browser profile, with each consent
      category both accepted and rejected. Check all pages, forms, embeds,
      booking links, tag managers and external scripts. Replace every example
      row above with verified information.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by["browser-controls"]}>

    <p>
      Most browsers allow you to view, delete or block cookies through their
      settings. You can also use private browsing modes, clear site data or
      configure browser extensions that limit tracking.
    </p>

    <p>
      Browser controls can affect how websites work. Blocking all cookies may
      prevent parts of a website from functioning correctly, including sign-in,
      preference, security, booking or form features.
    </p>

    <p>
      Browser settings do not necessarily replace the choices available through
      this website’s cookie-preference control. Where you withdraw consent,
      optional cookies should be prevented or removed in accordance with the
      configuration of the relevant consent platform.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by["personal-data"]}>

    <p>
      Some cookies and similar technologies may collect or create information
      that counts as personal data, for example an online identifier, IP address,
      cookie ID, device information or browsing activity.
    </p>

    <p>
      Information about how personal data is collected, used, shared, retained
      and protected is set out in the <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.changes}>

    <p>
      This notice may be updated when cookies, similar technologies, suppliers,
      website features or legal requirements change. The latest version will be
      published on this page with its revision date.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where a change materially affects optional technologies or your choices,
      the cookie banner or preference centre should also be updated before the
      change is made live.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection section={s.by.contact}>

    <p>
      If you have a question about this notice, a technology listed here or your
      cookie choices, contact:
    </p>

    <ContactBlock name={siteLegal.controllerName} email={siteLegal.privacyEmail} />

    <p>
      For more information about your wider privacy rights, see the <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <Fragment slot="draft-note">
    <p>
      <strong>Before publishing:</strong> run a proper cookie audit and replace
      every TODO. Your published notice, consent banner and actual scripts must
      match. Do not describe consent, opt-out, analytics minimisation, essential
      cookies or third-party tools unless they are configured and working as
      stated. The ICO requires clear, comprehensive information about cookies
      and their purposes, and consent for non-essential technologies unless a
      specific exemption applies.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</LegalNoticeLayout>

